! BRANCH RELATIVE (BR), Reference Manual p.40.
!
! THE DEFINITION, verbatim: "This instruction causes the absolute value
! of the effective address to be added to the instruction counter
! contents.  Bits 0-18 of the sum replace the instruction counter
! contents."
!
! HOW THIS BLOCK CAME ABOUT.  audit_opcode_coverage.py found BR listed
! among the implemented unconditional branches and executed as an
! ABSOLUTE branch identical to B -- contradicted by the X22-6533 pocket
! card, which gives B 010000000 and BR 011000000 as different operation
! codes and names BR "Branch Relative".  It was then left UNIMPLEMENTED
! (setting OP) rather than guessed at, because the base, sign and units
! were not established.  p.40 establishes all three, so it is now
! implemented and this program replaces br_unimplemented.strap.
!
! THE BASE IS THE STEPPED INSTRUCTION COUNTER.  p.39: "After an
! instruction is fetched, but before its execution, the instruction
! counter contents are stepped in ordinary fashion... prior to any
! attempt to replace the instruction counter contents."  So during
! execution the counter already holds the FALL-THROUGH address.
!
! CASE 1 IS THE MANUAL'S OWN WORKED EXAMPLE.  p.42: "A simple storage
! of the instruction counter without branching action is accomplished
! by STORE INSTRUCTION COUNTER IF BRANCH RELATIVE WITH A BRANCH ADDRESS
! OF 0."  A displacement of zero must therefore land on the
! fall-through -- no branching action -- which is only possible if the
! base is the stepped counter.  That sentence is what made the absolute
! reading impossible: under it, BR,0 would branch to 0.0, which p.39
! separately calls an invalid branching address.
!
! NOT SIGNED.  p.40 takes the ABSOLUTE VALUE, and p.20 says the address
! part of an instruction "is interpreted as a positive unsigned field".
! A sign can only enter through index modification, and BR discards it.
! This program uses no index, so it does not exercise that clause --
! see the note in run_tests.sh.
!
! UNITS ARE HALF-WORDS: the instruction counter is 19 bits and its
! nineteenth bit selects the half-word, and p.40 says "bits 0-18 of the
! sum" replace it.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED: hand-written from p.40 and p.42, not
! transcribed.  No deck block found so far uses BR with a non-zero
! displacement.

        SLC, (8)4000.0
!
! ---- case 1: a displacement of ZERO is no branching action ---------
START   BR, 0                        ! target = the stepped counter itself
        B, C2                        ! the fall-through: where it must land
        BEW, $                       ! hang up: BR went somewhere else
!
! ---- case 2: a displacement of TWO half-words ----------------------
C2      BR, 2                        ! target = fall-through + 2
        BEW, $                       ! fall-through + 0: hang up, no branch
        BEW, $                       ! fall-through + 1: hang up, short
        B, PASS                      ! fall-through + 2: the correct landing
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
